1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
Bread, the staff of life.

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Whether baked, fried, or boiled,

3
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
is the basic source of nourishment for half the world.

4
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:12,000
Grill it, roast it, slice it, toast it.

5
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:15,000
It's a wonder that two ingredients, flour and water,

6
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:19,000
can be transformed into so many different shapes and tastes.

7
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:21,000
Beautiful.

8
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:23,000
White bread may still be king in the U.S.,

9
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:26,000
but other breads are closing in.

10
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:31,000
Now, the untold story of bread on Modern Marvels.

11
00:00:43,160 --> 00:00:47,360
Call it pan or pain, ecmec or brout,

12
00:00:47,361 --> 00:00:51,000
every culture has its bread.

13
00:00:51,001 --> 00:00:54,000
It started with wheat in the Middle East about 10,000 years

14
00:00:54,001 --> 00:00:57,300
years ago, when someone left their porridge by the fire

15
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:01,900
and it dried into a thick cracker.

16
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,340
By 5000 BC, the Egyptians had discovered how

17
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:07,640
to make that cracker rise.

18
00:01:07,740 --> 00:01:09,740
Bread became the coin of the realm

19
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:12,760
and was payment for those who worked on the pyramids.

20
00:01:12,860 --> 00:01:15,820
The ancient Greeks developed 70 kinds of bread

21
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:17,220
and were the first to go on record

22
00:01:17,320 --> 00:01:21,300
as having a preference for the sweet taste of white bread.

23
00:01:21,301 --> 00:01:25,340
That preference continues today in the US, one of the biggest

24
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:27,640
bread-eating nations in the world.

25
00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:31,480
The average American eats 53 pounds every year, mostly

26
00:01:31,580 --> 00:01:33,780
white bread, mostly this one.

27
00:01:33,880 --> 00:01:35,620
Ooh, that's smart.

28
00:01:35,720 --> 00:01:36,920
Wonder-rich bread.

29
00:01:37,020 --> 00:01:40,280
What squeeze tells you why people call us the fresh guy?

30
00:01:40,380 --> 00:01:42,120
Oh!

31
00:01:42,220 --> 00:01:43,820
Wonder Bread is the biggest selling

32
00:01:43,920 --> 00:01:47,400
bread in America, produced by the biggest bakery in the nation,

33
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:51,300
Interstate Bakeries Corporation, or IBC.

34
00:01:51,301 --> 00:01:55,100
It's manufactured in over 25 regional IBC facilities

35
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:59,040
and shipped to over 155,000 markets.

36
00:01:59,140 --> 00:02:03,380
353 million loaves translate to about 3 and 1

37
00:02:03,480 --> 00:02:05,420
half billion sandwiches.

38
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:07,880
At one of their largest and most modern bakeries

39
00:02:07,980 --> 00:02:11,060
in Lexana, Kansas, IBC employs the latest

40
00:02:11,160 --> 00:02:13,820
in manufacturing technology on a massive scale.

41
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:19,820
Three tall bins you see right behind me

42
00:02:19,821 --> 00:02:25,120
are filled with white flour that we use for making Wonder Bread.

43
00:02:25,220 --> 00:02:28,300
They hold about 150,000 pounds each.

44
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:34,360
And we go through roughly about 840,000 pounds in a week.

45
00:02:34,460 --> 00:02:36,300
The creation of a loaf of Wonder Bread

46
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:39,300
is a symphony of speed and efficiency.

47
00:02:39,400 --> 00:02:43,140
From start to finish, it takes just 6 and 1 half hours.

48
00:02:43,240 --> 00:02:46,320
First, sugar, yeast, and flour are mixed with water

49
00:02:46,321 --> 00:02:53,100
into a 1,000-pound ball called a sponge and left to rise.

50
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:58,000
In three hours, the yeast makes the dough double in size.

51
00:02:58,100 --> 00:03:01,760
Next, the sponge is dropped into a one-ton mixer.

52
00:03:01,860 --> 00:03:03,800
More flour, yeast, sugar, and water

53
00:03:03,900 --> 00:03:05,900
are piped in from the silos.

54
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,040
5% of the mixture is liquid sugar,

55
00:03:08,140 --> 00:03:13,140
which gives Wonder Bread its popular, slightly sweet taste.

56
00:03:13,240 --> 00:03:15,320
Additional ingredients like dough conditioners

57
00:03:15,321 --> 00:03:21,000
preservative enzymes and mold inhibitors are added by hand.

58
00:03:21,001 --> 00:03:23,560
But it's the enriched flour of Wonder Bread that has been its

59
00:03:23,561 --> 00:03:28,000
calling card. In 1941, the US government asked

60
00:03:28,001 --> 00:03:31,560
food manufacturers to voluntarily add 8B vitamins to their

61
00:03:31,561 --> 00:03:36,280
products to prevent diseases caused by malnutrition.

62
00:03:36,281 --> 00:03:40,680
Wonder Bread agreed. It gave the nutrition that they

63
00:03:40,681 --> 00:03:45,320
needed to virtually eliminate those diseases and because it

64
00:03:45,321 --> 00:03:50,620
it evolved to a campaign that was build strong bodies.

65
00:03:50,720 --> 00:03:52,020
The enriched ingredients emerge

66
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:55,100
after 12 and 1 1 minutes in the mixer

67
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:59,740
as over 2,000 pounds of wonder dough.

68
00:03:59,840 --> 00:04:02,040
For the next 3 and 1 1 hours, the dough

69
00:04:02,140 --> 00:04:04,800
will be constantly on the move, virtually untouched

70
00:04:04,900 --> 00:04:05,800
by human hands.

71
00:04:12,940 --> 00:04:15,280
The half-filled bread pans and a half-filled bread

72
00:04:15,320 --> 00:04:18,160
a warm, humid bulb, where they circulate slowly

73
00:04:18,260 --> 00:04:20,160
for the next hour.

74
00:04:20,260 --> 00:04:22,260
This is called the proofing stage,

75
00:04:22,360 --> 00:04:25,500
because as the dough rises, it is proof that the yeast is active

76
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:27,560
and doing its job.

77
00:04:27,660 --> 00:04:29,160
Inside the dough, the yeast cells

78
00:04:29,260 --> 00:04:32,140
begin to feed on the dough's sugars and metabolize.

79
00:04:32,240 --> 00:04:33,640
The byproducts of this interaction

80
00:04:33,740 --> 00:04:36,200
are carbon dioxide and alcohol.

81
00:04:36,300 --> 00:04:40,080
As the carbon dioxide expands, it becomes trapped by the dough,

82
00:04:40,180 --> 00:04:42,180
causing it to rise.

83
00:04:42,280 --> 00:04:44,920
The rising continues until the sugars are consumed,

84
00:04:44,921 --> 00:04:48,060
or until the yeast is killed by the heat of the oven.

85
00:04:48,160 --> 00:04:51,160
Much of the alcohol is devoured by bacteria in the dough,

86
00:04:51,260 --> 00:04:53,920
contributing to the signature taste of the bread.

87
00:04:54,020 --> 00:04:56,660
The rest will be burned off in the oven.

88
00:04:56,760 --> 00:04:58,500
Right here, you're looking at our oven

89
00:04:58,600 --> 00:04:59,940
that we baked the product in.

90
00:05:00,040 --> 00:05:04,540
It's approximately 175 feet long, has eight zones in it.

91
00:05:04,640 --> 00:05:08,240
We bake for an average of about 18, 18 and a half minutes.

92
00:05:08,241 --> 00:05:13,240
Here's our bread being de-panned.

93
00:05:13,340 --> 00:05:16,140
There's these suction cups with about 20 pounds of vacuum.

94
00:05:16,240 --> 00:05:19,380
They'll lift it out of that pan, set it on the belt,

95
00:05:19,480 --> 00:05:21,880
and it travels up into our cooler.

96
00:05:21,980 --> 00:05:23,780
At this stage, the interior of the loaf

97
00:05:23,880 --> 00:05:26,800
is over 200 degrees Fahrenheit.

98
00:05:26,900 --> 00:05:28,900
It will need to be cooled to 100 degrees

99
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:31,000
before it can be sliced and wrapped.

100
00:05:31,100 --> 00:05:34,140
Wrapping it too soon would cause condensation in the package,

101
00:05:34,240 --> 00:05:37,040
ruin the crust, and the crust.

102
00:05:37,041 --> 00:05:39,040
the crust, and promote mold growth.

103
00:05:39,140 --> 00:05:41,880
It's been six and a half hours since we started making this

104
00:05:41,980 --> 00:05:44,380
bread that's right behind me here.

105
00:05:44,480 --> 00:05:46,580
It's in the final process of wrapping.

106
00:05:46,680 --> 00:05:49,480
It gets sliced and then packaged, put on a rack,

107
00:05:49,580 --> 00:05:52,080
sent to our shipping department.

108
00:05:52,180 --> 00:05:54,800
In 1930, Wonder Bread was one of the first brands

109
00:05:54,900 --> 00:05:56,900
to slice its loaves.

110
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:58,800
Initially, the public was skeptical,

111
00:05:58,900 --> 00:06:02,200
fearing that sliced bread would go stale too quickly.

112
00:06:02,300 --> 00:06:04,400
But it gradually caught on.

113
00:06:04,500 --> 00:06:06,980
Today, it's a sign of high praise to be the best

114
00:06:07,040 --> 00:06:09,440
in sliced bread.

115
00:06:09,540 --> 00:06:13,480
Wonder Bread's name was invented in 1921 when a baking company

116
00:06:13,580 --> 00:06:16,040
executive went to a hot air balloon exhibition.

117
00:06:16,140 --> 00:06:19,480
And he looked up and he saw, with a sense of wonder,

118
00:06:19,580 --> 00:06:20,980
all of these balloons.

119
00:06:21,080 --> 00:06:23,620
And it occurred to him, what a great name for a product.

120
00:06:23,720 --> 00:06:25,900
And that's how Wonder Bread got its name.

121
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,600
It's also why the red, yellow, and blue balloons are still

122
00:06:29,700 --> 00:06:32,040
an icon on the label.

123
00:06:32,140 --> 00:06:34,140
Wonder Bread is baked five days a week

124
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:36,400
and delivered the day after it's baked.

125
00:06:36,401 --> 00:06:39,440
The baking days are indicated by the color of the packing ties,

126
00:06:39,540 --> 00:06:43,600
blue, green, red, white, or yellow.

127
00:06:43,700 --> 00:06:45,700
White bread continues to be the biggest selling bread

128
00:06:45,800 --> 00:06:51,240
in America, and Wonder Bread is the leader of that pack.

129
00:06:51,340 --> 00:06:54,060
Every loaf of Wonder Bread, and most other American breads

130
00:06:54,160 --> 00:06:56,560
as well, starts in a wheat field.

131
00:06:56,660 --> 00:06:59,660
100,000 square miles of wheat are harvested every year

132
00:06:59,760 --> 00:07:05,200
in the US, an area equal to the entire state of Colorado.

133
00:07:05,201 --> 00:07:07,700
Every grain of wheat has three elements,

134
00:07:07,800 --> 00:07:09,640
the protective outer layer called bran,

135
00:07:09,740 --> 00:07:12,200
which contains vitamins and fiber.

136
00:07:12,300 --> 00:07:14,940
The germ, which is the embryo of a new wheat plant,

137
00:07:15,040 --> 00:07:17,440
is made up of oils and minerals.

138
00:07:17,540 --> 00:07:19,840
And the endosperm, which provides the nutrients

139
00:07:19,940 --> 00:07:23,660
for a new plant, mostly protein and starch.

140
00:07:23,760 --> 00:07:26,760
White flour is pure, starchy endosperm.

141
00:07:26,860 --> 00:07:29,320
Separating that out has been the goal of every flour mill

142
00:07:29,420 --> 00:07:32,060
since the beginning days of bread making.

143
00:07:32,160 --> 00:07:34,200
One of the most modern mills in America

144
00:07:34,201 --> 00:07:38,080
is the Hal Ross Mill at Kansas State University in Manhattan, Kansas.

145
00:07:39,340 --> 00:07:42,920
Here, students and professors experiment with the latest in milling technology

146
00:07:42,921 --> 00:07:45,160
in a full-scale functioning flour mill.

147
00:07:45,880 --> 00:07:49,640
This wheat that we're going to mill today is hard red winter,

148
00:07:50,020 --> 00:07:53,720
and it gets its name because the outside of the berry is reddish brown,

149
00:07:54,220 --> 00:07:56,960
and the inside of the berry is hard and vitreous.

150
00:07:57,120 --> 00:08:02,280
And so about 75% of the inside part of that kernel we can turn into white flour.

151
00:08:02,281 --> 00:08:05,180
The rest of it ends up in a byproduct, either as feed

152
00:08:05,280 --> 00:08:07,220
or maybe as bran in a breakfast cereal

153
00:08:07,320 --> 00:08:09,380
or a bran muffin or something like that.

154
00:08:09,480 --> 00:08:11,520
Today's mills are an elaborate labyrinth

155
00:08:11,620 --> 00:08:13,620
of pressurized tubes and equipment

156
00:08:13,720 --> 00:08:16,820
that move the grain particles from floor to floor.

157
00:08:16,920 --> 00:08:19,240
The grinding operation begins on the first floor,

158
00:08:19,340 --> 00:08:22,540
where stainless steel rollers do the work of the ancient millstones.

159
00:08:22,640 --> 00:08:27,140
The whole kernels are coming down, being fed in

160
00:08:27,240 --> 00:08:29,280
to what we call a double high roller mill.

161
00:08:29,281 --> 00:08:33,720
We've got two sets of big steel rolls, one on top of the other

162
00:08:33,820 --> 00:08:37,820
that this product is passing through to be broken apart to

163
00:08:37,920 --> 00:08:42,920
try to remove these chunks of endosperm from the kernel.

164
00:08:43,020 --> 00:08:45,840
We've got here a five-story building.

165
00:08:45,940 --> 00:08:48,940
We've got equipment spread across all floors.

166
00:08:49,040 --> 00:08:52,640
As we lift the particles by negative air up to the top floor

167
00:08:52,740 --> 00:08:54,580
and then drop it into a sifter.

168
00:08:54,680 --> 00:08:57,440
Inside the vibrating sifter is a series of screens

169
00:08:57,441 --> 00:09:01,140
smaller and smaller apertures. The flakes of bran and the oily

170
00:09:01,141 --> 00:09:04,040
wheat germ particles are sifted out at the top levels.

171
00:09:04,041 --> 00:09:07,040
The smaller powdery particles of endosperm fall through to the

172
00:09:07,041 --> 00:09:09,980
bottom. On the large size, we have

173
00:09:09,981 --> 00:09:14,200
big bran flakes, and clear over on this side, we've got flour.

174
00:09:14,201 --> 00:09:17,500
So out of that mixture, we've separated the finest particles

175
00:09:17,501 --> 00:09:22,000
for flour from the largest. In the middle, we've got some

176
00:09:22,001 --> 00:09:24,800
particles that still need to be processed.

177
00:09:24,801 --> 00:09:28,800
Optic sensors isolate and remove defective grains of wheat.

178
00:09:28,900 --> 00:09:31,800
High-speed impact machines destroy any insect eggs or

179
00:09:31,900 --> 00:09:33,800
parts.

180
00:09:33,900 --> 00:09:36,740
And flour streams with distinct quality features allow the

181
00:09:36,840 --> 00:09:39,740
miller to make an infinite variety of flour mixes.

182
00:09:39,840 --> 00:09:45,760
Here we've got the ability to take samples from each of the

183
00:09:45,860 --> 00:09:48,800
different flour streams in the mill.

184
00:09:48,900 --> 00:09:51,800
Knowing how to mix the flour streams to get the best quality

185
00:09:51,801 --> 00:09:55,800
quality white flour requires more than just a good eye.

186
00:09:55,900 --> 00:09:58,440
Across the Kansas State campus in the Bakery Science

187
00:09:58,540 --> 00:10:01,980
Laboratory, scientists have devised dozens of tests

188
00:10:02,080 --> 00:10:05,140
to check the quality of the flour and the bread

189
00:10:05,240 --> 00:10:06,840
that it bakes.

190
00:10:06,940 --> 00:10:10,460
The texture analyzer measures the firmness of baked bread.

191
00:10:10,560 --> 00:10:12,660
A machine called the extensograph

192
00:10:12,760 --> 00:10:16,360
tests how well the flour responds to kneading and mixing.

193
00:10:16,460 --> 00:10:19,300
And this device, the alveograph, tests

194
00:10:19,301 --> 00:10:23,300
will retain the carbon dioxide air bubbles created by the yeast

195
00:10:23,400 --> 00:10:25,940
by testing the quality of one of bread's key

196
00:10:26,040 --> 00:10:28,540
and unique components, gluten.

197
00:10:28,640 --> 00:10:32,640
Wheat contains a protein called gluten protein.

198
00:10:32,740 --> 00:10:35,880
It's the only cereal grain that contains gluten.

199
00:10:35,980 --> 00:10:40,620
When it develops, gluten forms into a cohesive mass.

200
00:10:40,720 --> 00:10:43,020
That is able to retain gas.

201
00:10:43,120 --> 00:10:46,320
So when you're making bread, the yeast is fermenting,

202
00:10:46,420 --> 00:10:47,920
it's producing gas.

203
00:10:47,921 --> 00:10:52,720
this gluten protein can hold that gas inside the dough.

204
00:10:52,820 --> 00:10:55,160
That's why your dough can raise to give you

205
00:10:55,260 --> 00:10:57,560
a nice expanded loaf of bread.

206
00:10:57,660 --> 00:11:00,460
This ability to retain gases and to rise

207
00:11:00,560 --> 00:11:03,600
is unique to wheat flour and was recorded by the Egyptians

208
00:11:03,700 --> 00:11:05,440
around 5000 BC.

209
00:11:05,540 --> 00:11:07,500
It was completely, completely luck.

210
00:11:07,600 --> 00:11:10,580
Somebody was having a porridge or a gruel

211
00:11:10,680 --> 00:11:13,620
that they left out too long, prehistoric,

212
00:11:13,720 --> 00:11:17,380
Paleolithic times, and wild yeast got in there

213
00:11:17,381 --> 00:11:19,780
and they left a little more, and they noticed that it started

214
00:11:19,880 --> 00:11:20,780
bubbling.

215
00:11:20,880 --> 00:11:22,280
And when they baked it, it wasn't dense and hard.

216
00:11:22,380 --> 00:11:24,180
It was light and expanded.

217
00:11:24,280 --> 00:11:26,920
They had unlocked the power of the yeast spores that occur

218
00:11:27,020 --> 00:11:29,920
naturally on grain and in the air.

219
00:11:30,020 --> 00:11:33,220
For the next 7,000 years, bakers learned to hold back

220
00:11:33,320 --> 00:11:36,440
a portion of their fermented dough, called the mother dough,

221
00:11:36,540 --> 00:11:39,340
to mix with additional flour and water, creating new batches

222
00:11:39,440 --> 00:11:40,840
of yeasted dough.

223
00:11:40,940 --> 00:11:43,880
But the actual science behind leavening bread remained a

224
00:11:43,881 --> 00:11:47,780
until 1859, when Louis Pasteur discovered that yeast

225
00:11:47,880 --> 00:11:50,380
was a single-cell living organism.

226
00:11:50,480 --> 00:11:52,260
With that knowledge, scientists soon

227
00:11:52,360 --> 00:11:55,360
learned how to grow yeast separate from the dough.

228
00:11:55,460 --> 00:11:58,920
This sparked a revolution in bread production.

229
00:11:59,020 --> 00:12:00,460
At the Fleischmann's yeast factories

230
00:12:00,560 --> 00:12:03,460
in Memphis, Tennessee, and Montreal, Canada,

231
00:12:03,560 --> 00:12:06,840
new life is created every second of the day.

232
00:12:06,940 --> 00:12:09,580
Selected yeast microbes are mixed into vats of yeast's

233
00:12:09,680 --> 00:12:13,880
favorite food, warm, unrefined molasses.

234
00:12:13,881 --> 00:12:17,680
a byproduct of sugar production that would otherwise go to waste

235
00:12:17,780 --> 00:12:20,980
if it weren't perfect for developing bread yeast.

236
00:12:21,080 --> 00:12:24,760
This fermentation process is very similar to brewing beer.

237
00:12:24,860 --> 00:12:27,420
The difference is, once the 36-hour fermentation

238
00:12:27,520 --> 00:12:30,560
is complete, Fleischmann's throws out the alcohol

239
00:12:30,660 --> 00:12:33,700
and keeps the yeast.

240
00:12:33,800 --> 00:12:36,900
Yeast responds to the slightest changes in environment,

241
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:41,000
so scientists monitor every batch.

242
00:12:41,001 --> 00:12:44,440
Fermentation produces a brew called liquid cream yeast,

243
00:12:44,540 --> 00:12:46,480
which is shipped out in tankers and trains

244
00:12:46,580 --> 00:12:48,700
to bakeries across the country.

245
00:12:48,800 --> 00:12:51,680
But some is destined for home use.

246
00:12:51,780 --> 00:12:53,440
Sheets of cream yeast are further

247
00:12:53,540 --> 00:12:58,380
dried on stainless steel drums and scraped to make cake yeast.

248
00:12:58,480 --> 00:13:00,620
Additionally, the cake yeast can be broken down

249
00:13:00,720 --> 00:13:03,920
into granules and slowly dried.

250
00:13:04,020 --> 00:13:06,820
This process, invented by Fleischmanns in the 1940s,

251
00:13:06,920 --> 00:13:09,500
causes the yeast to go dormant.

252
00:13:09,501 --> 00:13:12,600
This is called dry yeast and can last up to two years

253
00:13:12,700 --> 00:13:15,440
in this form or until it's mixed with warm water

254
00:13:15,540 --> 00:13:19,640
and brought back to life to make bread rise.

255
00:13:19,740 --> 00:13:21,940
Though most commercial bakeries use some form

256
00:13:22,040 --> 00:13:24,840
of concentrated yeast, San Francisco sourdough

257
00:13:24,940 --> 00:13:27,360
is still made the old-fashioned way

258
00:13:27,460 --> 00:13:30,960
with stinky fermented mother dough.

259
00:13:31,060 --> 00:13:32,460
It just gets you so strong.

260
00:13:36,360 --> 00:13:39,060
The story of American bread is the story of immigrants

261
00:13:39,061 --> 00:13:42,160
and the blending of cultures.

262
00:13:42,260 --> 00:13:45,200
Native Americans, European settlers, and African slaves

263
00:13:45,300 --> 00:13:48,300
all brought their baking traditions to this country.

264
00:13:48,400 --> 00:13:52,600
The mix gave rise to some uniquely American breads.

265
00:13:52,700 --> 00:13:54,080
There are few places in the world

266
00:13:54,180 --> 00:13:55,640
where the local bread is so singular

267
00:13:55,740 --> 00:13:58,580
that it becomes synonymous with the city of its origin.

268
00:13:58,680 --> 00:14:00,820
But when it comes to sourdough, everyone

269
00:14:00,920 --> 00:14:04,620
knows the city is San Francisco.

270
00:14:04,720 --> 00:14:06,180
Along the docks of Fisherman's Wharf

271
00:14:06,280 --> 00:14:08,820
is a new building that houses the oldest bakeries

272
00:14:08,821 --> 00:14:12,520
bakery in town, Boudin Sourdough Bakery.

273
00:14:12,620 --> 00:14:14,500
They've been using the same sourdough starter

274
00:14:14,600 --> 00:14:18,000
for the last 160 years.

275
00:14:18,100 --> 00:14:21,600
I'm going to show you something that if I lose these,

276
00:14:21,700 --> 00:14:23,300
I have to shut the doors.

277
00:14:23,400 --> 00:14:26,640
I cannot make sourdough bread anymore if I lose these.

278
00:14:26,740 --> 00:14:28,000
But of course, we will not do that.

279
00:14:28,100 --> 00:14:32,140
But actually, I'm going to show you how it looks like.

280
00:14:32,240 --> 00:14:37,120
This piece of dough dates back to 1849.

281
00:14:37,121 --> 00:14:40,120
I've been taking care of it almost for the past 30 years.

282
00:14:40,220 --> 00:14:42,120
That's been my job, the caretaker of this baby.

283
00:14:42,620 --> 00:14:47,120
These pots of fermented dough were created by mixing one part pre-fermented mother dough

284
00:14:47,121 --> 00:14:50,860
with 20 parts flour and water and left to sit for one day.

285
00:14:51,460 --> 00:14:52,260
Okay, look at this.

286
00:14:52,620 --> 00:14:55,780
It's just a piece of dough yesterday that started very small.

287
00:14:56,340 --> 00:14:59,940
This is what flavors of sourdough, the famous San Francisco sourdough bread.

288
00:15:00,160 --> 00:15:01,000
Look at this, look at this.

289
00:15:01,280 --> 00:15:02,180
You break it up.

290
00:15:04,780 --> 00:15:06,540
It just gets you so strong.

291
00:15:06,541 --> 00:15:13,040
So I'm gonna throw it in here, and it's ready to go.

292
00:15:13,140 --> 00:15:15,880
Sourdough bread requires no additional yeast

293
00:15:15,980 --> 00:15:18,860
because the mother dough is alive with local San Francisco

294
00:15:18,960 --> 00:15:20,380
yeast spores.

295
00:15:20,480 --> 00:15:23,560
The weather here in San Francisco is so special.

296
00:15:23,660 --> 00:15:27,700
There is the spores go into this flour and water.

297
00:15:27,800 --> 00:15:29,400
It makes the special flavor.

298
00:15:29,500 --> 00:15:32,400
We only have these spores in this area.

299
00:15:32,500 --> 00:15:35,200
So if you're trying to steal the piece, go ahead, take it.

300
00:15:35,300 --> 00:15:36,540
You can take it.

301
00:15:36,541 --> 00:15:38,000
I mean, we don't let you do that.

302
00:15:38,100 --> 00:15:41,680
But if you take it, if you take it back to New York,

303
00:15:41,780 --> 00:15:44,040
then it's going to become a New York sourdough

304
00:15:44,140 --> 00:15:48,380
in about 30 days, because you cannot take the weather back

305
00:15:48,480 --> 00:15:50,480
to New York.

306
00:15:50,580 --> 00:15:52,080
20 pounds of mother dough is enough

307
00:15:52,180 --> 00:15:56,860
to provide the yeast for a 200-pound batch of sourdough.

308
00:15:56,960 --> 00:15:58,560
Once the dough gets mixed, we're

309
00:15:58,660 --> 00:16:00,860
going to check to see if the dough is mixed correctly.

310
00:16:00,960 --> 00:16:04,600
The way you could tell is you stretch it very, very, very

311
00:16:04,700 --> 00:16:06,000
thin, very thin, very thin.

312
00:16:06,001 --> 00:16:06,900
You see that?

313
00:16:06,901 --> 00:16:09,140
How it's going to be almost transparent, OK?

314
00:16:09,141 --> 00:16:11,340
That's how you can tell it's well-developed.

315
00:16:11,341 --> 00:16:12,041
You see that?

316
00:16:12,042 --> 00:16:12,742
See that?

317
00:16:12,743 --> 00:16:14,240
You can always see my face right through.

318
00:16:14,241 --> 00:16:16,440
See that?

319
00:16:16,441 --> 00:16:19,540
Once every 20 minutes, a tub of mixed dough,

320
00:16:19,541 --> 00:16:23,560
enough for 400 loaves of bread, is dropped down to a divider

321
00:16:23,561 --> 00:16:26,360
that cuts it into chunks, perfectly sized

322
00:16:26,361 --> 00:16:28,520
for a specific type of loaf.

323
00:16:28,521 --> 00:16:31,600
A conical shaper then rolls it into a ball that is carried

324
00:16:31,601 --> 00:16:35,060
by conveyor belt to a resting basket, where the dough can do

325
00:16:35,061 --> 00:16:36,960
rise.

326
00:16:37,060 --> 00:16:39,060
After a half hour in the rotating basket,

327
00:16:39,160 --> 00:16:41,660
the raised dough goes through a forming stage,

328
00:16:41,760 --> 00:16:43,660
where it's given its next shape.

329
00:16:43,760 --> 00:16:46,800
Breads are hand-stretched and gently shaped before they are

330
00:16:46,900 --> 00:16:49,700
wheeled into a cool, humidified closet.

331
00:16:49,800 --> 00:16:53,620
Once the dough gets mixed and shaped and stretched,

332
00:16:53,720 --> 00:16:56,720
now it goes away for another day.

333
00:16:56,820 --> 00:16:58,720
So we put it in here.

334
00:16:58,820 --> 00:17:01,720
We won't bake it until tomorrow.

335
00:17:05,060 --> 00:17:08,260
Throughout the day at Boudin, tourists from around the world

336
00:17:08,360 --> 00:17:10,800
line up with locals to shop for this one of a kind

337
00:17:10,900 --> 00:17:11,800
sourdough bread.

338
00:17:11,900 --> 00:17:15,400
Hello, and welcome to Boudin at the Wharf.

339
00:17:15,500 --> 00:17:17,200
And many come to the bakery museum

340
00:17:17,300 --> 00:17:19,400
to hear the tale of an immigrant baker who

341
00:17:19,500 --> 00:17:21,640
was in the right place at the right time.

342
00:17:21,740 --> 00:17:25,320
So the real story of Boudin is the gold rush.

343
00:17:25,420 --> 00:17:28,220
Now, a lot of people came to dig for gold,

344
00:17:28,320 --> 00:17:32,720
but a lot of people came to so-called mine the miners.

345
00:17:32,721 --> 00:17:34,880
And one year after the gold rush,

346
00:17:34,980 --> 00:17:38,060
there were 70 bakeries in San Francisco.

347
00:17:38,160 --> 00:17:39,820
And Boudin was one of them.

348
00:17:39,920 --> 00:17:41,960
Boudin is the only one that survived.

349
00:17:42,060 --> 00:17:44,960
And it's the oldest continuously operating

350
00:17:45,060 --> 00:17:46,860
business in the city.

351
00:17:46,960 --> 00:17:48,640
The Boudins eventually sold the bakery

352
00:17:48,740 --> 00:17:50,280
to another immigrant baker.

353
00:17:50,380 --> 00:17:54,280
My father, Steve Girauda, came over in 1935 from Italy

354
00:17:54,380 --> 00:17:55,480
to San Francisco.

355
00:17:55,580 --> 00:17:57,180
And his first job was at Boudin Bakery.

356
00:17:57,280 --> 00:17:59,580
And everybody talks about artisan bread today.

357
00:17:59,581 --> 00:18:02,580
My father was one of San Francisco's authentic and

358
00:18:02,680 --> 00:18:04,480
original artisan bakers.

359
00:18:04,580 --> 00:18:06,780
But as he made his bread, we'd go to school

360
00:18:06,880 --> 00:18:07,880
and we'd have sandwiches.

361
00:18:07,980 --> 00:18:11,120
And we'd sit down at a bench in the schoolyard with all the kids

362
00:18:11,220 --> 00:18:14,820
that were eating peanut butter and jelly and, I don't know,

363
00:18:14,920 --> 00:18:16,340
mayonnaise sandwiches.

364
00:18:16,440 --> 00:18:18,040
They'd look at, on white bread, they'd look at us,

365
00:18:18,140 --> 00:18:20,140
because the mustard would be falling out of our sandwiches

366
00:18:20,240 --> 00:18:22,140
because the bread had holes in it.

367
00:18:22,240 --> 00:18:24,880
That bread with holes is now flying off the shelves

368
00:18:24,980 --> 00:18:26,380
at Boudin.

369
00:18:26,480 --> 00:18:28,780
But despite the increasing demand, each loaf still

370
00:18:28,781 --> 00:18:31,320
still gets hands-on attention.

371
00:18:31,420 --> 00:18:34,980
I'm scoring the bread that is going to go into the oven.

372
00:18:35,080 --> 00:18:36,780
After those three days that we talked about,

373
00:18:36,880 --> 00:18:39,020
this is the final step before it gets baked.

374
00:18:39,120 --> 00:18:41,060
It gets scored for looks.

375
00:18:41,160 --> 00:18:44,820
At the same time, it gets scored to release the gases

376
00:18:44,920 --> 00:18:46,900
so the bread comes out nice and even.

377
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:48,500
Otherwise, if we just throw it in like this,

378
00:18:48,600 --> 00:18:51,740
it's going to burst to the weakest part of the skin.

379
00:18:51,840 --> 00:18:57,780
We got an oven that varies from about 400 to about 440 degrees.

380
00:18:57,781 --> 00:18:59,780
And then you also have to steam it.

381
00:18:59,781 --> 00:19:03,320
If you don't give it steam, it's going to look all gray.

382
00:19:03,321 --> 00:19:04,780
But this will give it a nice crust.

383
00:19:04,781 --> 00:19:07,920
And actually, it will make it expand even more in the oven.

384
00:19:07,921 --> 00:19:09,500
You'll see that right now.

385
00:19:09,501 --> 00:19:11,760
Average time is around 20 to 30 minutes,

386
00:19:11,761 --> 00:19:13,760
depends on the size of the bread.

387
00:19:13,761 --> 00:19:16,500
And then press start.

388
00:19:16,501 --> 00:19:18,600
You'll feel the steam.

389
00:19:18,601 --> 00:19:20,340
You see the steam coming out right now?

390
00:19:20,341 --> 00:19:24,040
Yeah, yeah.

391
00:19:24,041 --> 00:19:25,040
Good loaf of bread.

392
00:19:25,041 --> 00:19:27,840
See that nice crusty color?

393
00:19:27,841 --> 00:19:29,040
Ooh, hot.

394
00:19:29,041 --> 00:19:30,740
See that crunchy?

395
00:19:30,741 --> 00:19:32,040
It'll be chewy on the inside.

396
00:19:32,041 --> 00:19:34,040
The scores are very nice and even.

397
00:19:34,041 --> 00:19:35,540
You know, that means these lines here.

398
00:19:35,541 --> 00:19:38,040
And also, you know, you see the top it?

399
00:19:38,041 --> 00:19:39,040
Make sure it's hollow.

400
00:19:39,041 --> 00:19:43,040
If it was, you can feel it if it's still underdone.

401
00:19:43,041 --> 00:19:44,040
It's a different sound.

402
00:19:44,041 --> 00:19:47,040
You see the echo?

403
00:19:47,041 --> 00:19:48,040
Beautiful.

404
00:19:48,041 --> 00:19:50,040
And then, look at that.

405
00:19:50,041 --> 00:19:51,040
How crunchy.

406
00:19:51,041 --> 00:19:54,040
Take a piece of these.

407
00:19:55,040 --> 00:19:55,940
Mmm.

408
00:19:56,040 --> 00:19:59,340
Flour, water, and salt doesn't get any better than this.

409
00:19:59,440 --> 00:20:00,440
Ah, I love it.

410
00:20:00,540 --> 00:20:03,280
It makes my day when I see something that started three

411
00:20:03,380 --> 00:20:05,980
days ago and it comes out to this good.

412
00:20:06,080 --> 00:20:07,980
I'm happy.

413
00:20:08,080 --> 00:20:10,780
Wheat may be the basis for most breads,

414
00:20:10,880 --> 00:20:13,700
but corn is an American specialty.

415
00:20:13,800 --> 00:20:15,600
The grain that first grew on this continent

416
00:20:15,700 --> 00:20:17,100
has been a key source of nutrition

417
00:20:17,200 --> 00:20:19,740
for its Native American inhabitants.

418
00:20:19,840 --> 00:20:21,840
Today, we call it cornbread.

419
00:20:21,940 --> 00:20:23,800
And its centuries-old traditions are preserved

420
00:20:23,801 --> 00:20:27,800
like Big Mama's Southern Barbecue in Pasadena, California.

421
00:20:27,900 --> 00:20:28,800
Beautiful.

422
00:20:31,540 --> 00:20:33,240
Dargan McWhorter and his children

423
00:20:33,340 --> 00:20:35,540
run Big Mama's Restaurant.

424
00:20:35,640 --> 00:20:37,040
You know, I'm going to cut that thing

425
00:20:37,140 --> 00:20:38,540
good as your old daddy.

426
00:20:38,640 --> 00:20:40,760
It's named in honor of the family matriarch,

427
00:20:40,860 --> 00:20:44,060
Emma Sue McWhorter, who provided the original recipes.

428
00:20:44,160 --> 00:20:48,060
Big Mama is my mother and his grandmother.

429
00:20:48,160 --> 00:20:53,160
And she's from Big Mama was born in Georgia.

430
00:20:53,161 --> 00:20:57,160
She learned from her grandmother, who was part Cherokee.

431
00:20:57,260 --> 00:21:00,160
Are we ready to whip up a batch of some hot water cornbread?

432
00:21:00,260 --> 00:21:01,160
Yes.

433
00:21:01,260 --> 00:21:02,900
Southern cooking was heavily influenced

434
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,900
by Native American dishes, which relied on corn.

435
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:09,200
That looks like two cups of cornmeal.

436
00:21:09,300 --> 00:21:11,920
When the wheat-loving Europeans settled the continent,

437
00:21:12,020 --> 00:21:15,620
they disparagingly referred to cornbread as Indian bread.

438
00:21:15,720 --> 00:21:20,220
It became stigmatized, associated with hardship and poverty.

439
00:21:20,320 --> 00:21:22,220
This is hot water cornbread.

440
00:21:22,221 --> 00:21:26,820
Now, this has been passed down for centuries.

441
00:21:26,920 --> 00:21:28,620
Hot water cornbread is held together

442
00:21:28,720 --> 00:21:31,960
by superheating the cornmeal to make it congeal.

443
00:21:32,060 --> 00:21:34,860
To form the patties, the hands must be icy cold

444
00:21:34,960 --> 00:21:36,660
to keep it from sticking.

445
00:21:36,760 --> 00:21:38,360
Then it's fried.

446
00:21:38,460 --> 00:21:40,300
This soul food staple has its roots

447
00:21:40,400 --> 00:21:41,680
in Native American cooking.

448
00:21:41,780 --> 00:21:44,740
A lot of the Southern African Americans

449
00:21:44,840 --> 00:21:47,320
married into the Indian tribe.

450
00:21:47,420 --> 00:21:52,220
Soul food is a blend and mixture of everything.

451
00:21:52,221 --> 00:21:56,020
As cornbread became a staple of Southern cooking,

452
00:21:56,120 --> 00:21:57,760
new ingredients were gradually added,

453
00:21:57,860 --> 00:22:00,760
like pork fat, sugar, buttermilk, and eggs.

454
00:22:00,860 --> 00:22:03,160
Delicious.

455
00:22:03,260 --> 00:22:06,360
Ditto.

456
00:22:06,460 --> 00:22:10,980
Family recipes became jealously guarded secrets.

457
00:22:11,080 --> 00:22:12,780
And you couldn't get it.

458
00:22:12,880 --> 00:22:13,980
You couldn't get the recipe.

459
00:22:14,080 --> 00:22:16,080
They would not give it to you.

460
00:22:16,180 --> 00:22:18,980
All right, will you give me the recipe for your cornbread?

461
00:22:19,080 --> 00:22:19,980
No.

462
00:22:22,220 --> 00:22:25,220
We may never learn the recipe of Big Mama's cornbread,

463
00:22:25,320 --> 00:22:29,660
but another bread has had the same recipe for 2,000 years.

464
00:22:29,760 --> 00:22:32,560
Altar bread, also known as a communion wafer,

465
00:22:32,660 --> 00:22:35,000
is used in the sacrament of communion in the Catholic

466
00:22:35,100 --> 00:22:37,360
Church.

467
00:22:37,460 --> 00:22:41,200
It is made only of flour and water,

468
00:22:41,300 --> 00:22:44,380
reflecting the Last Supper in which Jesus broke unleavened

469
00:22:44,480 --> 00:22:45,680
bread with his disciples.

470
00:22:45,681 --> 00:22:51,080
disciples. Still, how it is made and where

471
00:22:51,081 --> 00:22:55,860
it is made are not widely known. In most of the world, nuns bake the

472
00:22:55,861 --> 00:22:59,920
holy wafers. But in the United States, 80%

473
00:22:59,921 --> 00:23:02,960
of the bread that is placed on the tongues of faithful Catholics is

474
00:23:02,961 --> 00:23:07,440
produced here. In the village of Greenville,

475
00:23:07,441 --> 00:23:10,840
Rhode Island, the Cavanaugh family has been baking altar bread for the

476
00:23:10,841 --> 00:23:14,140
last 40 years. Well, it started with my

477
00:23:14,141 --> 00:23:20,020
grandfather who was an inventor with about 120 patents to his

478
00:23:20,021 --> 00:23:21,820
name.

479
00:23:21,821 --> 00:23:24,720
Kavanaugh's parish priest asked him to invent a machine that

480
00:23:24,721 --> 00:23:28,580
local nuns could use to increase their production of altar bread.

481
00:23:28,581 --> 00:23:32,160
It was the 1950s when fewer and fewer American women were

482
00:23:32,161 --> 00:23:33,960
entering convents.

483
00:23:33,961 --> 00:23:37,460
By the 1960s, even with Kavanaugh's help, the remaining

484
00:23:37,461 --> 00:23:40,400
nuns could no longer keep up with baby boomer demand for the

485
00:23:40,401 --> 00:23:42,760
holy waivers.

486
00:23:42,761 --> 00:23:46,000
So it was at that time that Kavanaugh Company began baking

487
00:23:46,001 --> 00:23:51,680
and supplying the nuns or convents with the breads rather than the equipment

488
00:23:51,681 --> 00:23:55,960
that it was good for them, good for us, so it worked well.

489
00:23:57,040 --> 00:24:00,840
Kavanaugh Company has devised a way to make something beautiful and edible

490
00:24:00,841 --> 00:24:02,540
from just two ingredients.

491
00:24:04,720 --> 00:24:11,440
By canon law, we cannot put in any material other than flour and water.

492
00:24:11,441 --> 00:24:14,940
So we're really limited, but the equipment that we have

493
00:24:15,040 --> 00:24:19,680
is able to manufacture quite well with those requirements.

494
00:24:19,780 --> 00:24:21,620
The unleavened flour and water mixture

495
00:24:21,720 --> 00:24:23,920
is streamed onto heated baking sheets

496
00:24:24,020 --> 00:24:28,920
and baked for 95 seconds into a thin, rigid sheet of bread.

497
00:24:29,020 --> 00:24:31,620
The rotary die cut seals the edges of the bread

498
00:24:31,720 --> 00:24:33,900
so nothing can chip off.

499
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:37,360
A tumbler separates the large breads from the small.

500
00:24:37,460 --> 00:24:41,140
And like-sized wafers are sucked up into pressurized tubes.

501
00:24:41,141 --> 00:24:43,380
whisked away to packaging.

502
00:24:43,480 --> 00:24:46,080
Each day, Cavanaugh Company produces about 9 million

503
00:24:46,180 --> 00:24:48,040
pieces of altar bread.

504
00:24:48,140 --> 00:24:50,580
My favorite wafer by far is the 1 1 1 1

505
00:24:50,680 --> 00:24:51,780
1 Special.

506
00:24:51,880 --> 00:24:55,680
There are three separate, unique, beautiful designs done

507
00:24:55,780 --> 00:24:57,560
by my father and uncle.

508
00:24:57,660 --> 00:25:01,200
The bread also has increased wheat germ in it.

509
00:25:01,300 --> 00:25:04,300
It gives it extra flavor and extra color.

510
00:25:04,400 --> 00:25:08,600
When you do go up to the altar and receive,

511
00:25:08,601 --> 00:25:11,840
It's a much more significant wafer.

512
00:25:11,940 --> 00:25:15,000
And in a remarkable change in American preferences,

513
00:25:15,100 --> 00:25:18,740
whole wheat wafers account for over 60% of Kavanaugh's

514
00:25:18,840 --> 00:25:20,540
altar bread orders.

515
00:25:20,640 --> 00:25:22,640
Across the continent, another round bread

516
00:25:22,740 --> 00:25:26,160
is tumbling out of the oven, answering the burning question,

517
00:25:26,260 --> 00:25:28,260
how does the pita get its pocket?

518
00:25:32,860 --> 00:25:36,960
The earliest bread was flat, unleavened, and round.

519
00:25:36,961 --> 00:25:39,800
The early Greeks called it pita, and its popularity

520
00:25:39,900 --> 00:25:42,300
spread around the Mediterranean.

521
00:25:42,400 --> 00:25:44,900
Today in the US, pita is favored as a sandwich bread

522
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:49,200
because of its unique ability to form a pocket.

523
00:25:49,300 --> 00:25:53,720
Nobody can take a bread and go like this in the market,

524
00:25:53,820 --> 00:25:58,920
and then open it again like this, and be able to open it

525
00:25:59,020 --> 00:26:00,020
and use it.

526
00:26:00,120 --> 00:26:01,420
Nobody can do that.

527
00:26:01,520 --> 00:26:02,320
They have tried.

528
00:26:02,420 --> 00:26:04,360
A lot of people came and tried that,

529
00:26:04,460 --> 00:26:05,960
and they will never be able to do that

530
00:26:05,961 --> 00:26:09,800
to do that because they don't know what they're doing.

531
00:26:09,900 --> 00:26:12,700
Albert Poyagian seems to know what he's doing.

532
00:26:12,800 --> 00:26:14,660
He's built a successful pita bread business

533
00:26:14,760 --> 00:26:20,200
in Pacoima, California, that bakes something for everyone.

534
00:26:20,300 --> 00:26:23,520
Arab bread for the Arabs, kosher Masada brand pita

535
00:26:23,620 --> 00:26:26,980
for the Israelis, lavash for the Lebanese,

536
00:26:27,080 --> 00:26:29,380
and whole grain for the vegans.

537
00:26:29,480 --> 00:26:32,860
Albert grew up in Syria eating handmade pita.

538
00:26:32,960 --> 00:26:35,960
His goal has been to duplicate that taste in quantity.

539
00:26:35,961 --> 00:26:38,860
The process at Global Bakery begins with mixing a half ton

540
00:26:38,960 --> 00:26:41,860
of dough at a time.

541
00:26:41,960 --> 00:26:44,360
In a trough, the dough is then hoisted up

542
00:26:44,460 --> 00:26:46,500
and emptied into a machine that divides it

543
00:26:46,600 --> 00:26:49,500
and rolls it into perfect balls.

544
00:26:49,600 --> 00:26:52,040
Those are dropped into baskets to rest and ride

545
00:26:52,140 --> 00:26:55,240
for a 20-minute cycle while the gluten relaxes

546
00:26:55,340 --> 00:26:57,380
and the dough rises.

547
00:26:57,480 --> 00:27:00,360
Next comes the sheeting process, where the dough ball is rolled

548
00:27:00,460 --> 00:27:03,280
flat in one direction, making it oblong,

549
00:27:03,281 --> 00:27:07,520
and then at right angles to make it round again.

550
00:27:07,620 --> 00:27:09,520
After a period of proofing, the sheets of dough

551
00:27:09,620 --> 00:27:14,260
head to the oven for an intense 20 to 40 second bake at 700

552
00:27:14,360 --> 00:27:17,220
to 1,200 degrees Fahrenheit.

553
00:27:17,320 --> 00:27:21,300
This is where the magic of the pita pocket takes place.

554
00:27:21,400 --> 00:27:23,240
Oxidation on the surface of the dough

555
00:27:23,340 --> 00:27:25,440
seals the exterior of the bread.

556
00:27:25,540 --> 00:27:28,800
Inside, millions of tiny bubbles of carbon dioxide

557
00:27:28,900 --> 00:27:32,380
expand rapidly, filling with superheated steam,

558
00:27:32,381 --> 00:27:36,820
causing the bread to expand like a hot air balloon

559
00:27:36,920 --> 00:27:39,220
until it naturally springs a leak.

560
00:27:39,320 --> 00:27:40,620
Look at this one here.

561
00:27:40,720 --> 00:27:43,120
Each one of them would have an air vent in the bottom.

562
00:27:43,220 --> 00:27:46,520
That's where the air is going to come out.

563
00:27:46,620 --> 00:27:49,460
Don't ever put your fingers in front of the air vent,

564
00:27:49,560 --> 00:27:51,060
because the temperature is coming out.

565
00:27:51,160 --> 00:27:52,040
It's very hot.

566
00:27:52,140 --> 00:27:54,740
If you look at it, just put your hand there and push it.

567
00:27:54,840 --> 00:27:58,040
It's very, very hot.

568
00:27:58,140 --> 00:28:00,140
The steaming pita pockets begin to cool

569
00:28:00,141 --> 00:28:01,940
to cool as they head up the conveyor belt

570
00:28:02,040 --> 00:28:04,540
to circle the bakery.

571
00:28:04,640 --> 00:28:06,620
After the breads take a lap around the factory,

572
00:28:06,720 --> 00:28:09,040
workers gather them, inspect them, stack them,

573
00:28:09,140 --> 00:28:11,260
and send them along for wrapping and shipping

574
00:28:11,360 --> 00:28:12,360
to Globo's customers.

575
00:28:15,220 --> 00:28:17,660
We sent all the way to New York, to Florida.

576
00:28:17,760 --> 00:28:21,660
Sometimes we ship pita bread from here to New York.

577
00:28:21,760 --> 00:28:25,900
The airfare is 10 times more than the pita bread.

578
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:29,340
While pita is embraced by many ethnic cultures,

579
00:28:29,341 --> 00:28:31,480
A loaf of challah has special significance

580
00:28:31,580 --> 00:28:35,180
for religious Jews.

581
00:28:35,280 --> 00:28:37,480
It's Thursday evening, and things look quiet

582
00:28:37,580 --> 00:28:40,680
at Continental Bakery in Valley Village, California.

583
00:28:40,780 --> 00:28:43,220
But behind the scenes, a full crew

584
00:28:43,320 --> 00:28:46,620
will work late into the night to bake challah, a soft, sweet,

585
00:28:46,720 --> 00:28:50,200
woven bread traditionally made with lots of eggs.

586
00:28:50,300 --> 00:28:52,600
The loaves will be sold the next day for the Friday evening

587
00:28:52,700 --> 00:28:54,700
celebration of the Jewish Sabbath.

588
00:28:54,800 --> 00:28:55,600
Anything else for you?

589
00:28:55,700 --> 00:28:58,440
Yes, I want an egg challah slice, please.

590
00:28:58,441 --> 00:29:01,780
Continental is a kosher bakery and is regularly inspected

591
00:29:01,880 --> 00:29:05,440
by a rabbi to be sure it adheres to kosher rules.

592
00:29:05,540 --> 00:29:07,880
What makes a bakery kosher is the ingredients.

593
00:29:07,980 --> 00:29:08,980
Real good.

594
00:29:09,080 --> 00:29:11,680
Well, what does it mean, ingredients, kosher?

595
00:29:11,780 --> 00:29:14,920
God prescribes in the book of Leviticus chapter 11,

596
00:29:15,020 --> 00:29:16,400
says these are the foods he may eat,

597
00:29:16,500 --> 00:29:18,660
these are the foods he may not eat.

598
00:29:18,760 --> 00:29:22,060
The role of a rabbi is very simple.

599
00:29:22,160 --> 00:29:24,260
He comes in, he knows what is kosher,

600
00:29:24,360 --> 00:29:25,260
he knows what's not kosher.

601
00:29:25,360 --> 00:29:27,000
Real good stuff is all kosher.

602
00:29:27,001 --> 00:29:29,760
He'll just walk in here, and he just looks in the pantry.

603
00:29:29,860 --> 00:29:31,000
What are you using today?

604
00:29:31,100 --> 00:29:34,240
And you do not know when he is going to be showing up.

605
00:29:34,340 --> 00:29:36,000
Challah for the Sabbath celebration

606
00:29:36,100 --> 00:29:38,840
is always woven in the same way, from the same number

607
00:29:38,940 --> 00:29:40,940
of strands.

608
00:29:41,040 --> 00:29:44,960
At the moment, we are rolling the bread,

609
00:29:45,060 --> 00:29:47,820
kneading the bread, shaping the bread into strands

610
00:29:47,920 --> 00:29:49,660
for braiding.

611
00:29:49,760 --> 00:29:55,560
So you need six strands for each loaf.

612
00:29:55,561 --> 00:29:59,400
So we are shaping them in groups of sixes.

613
00:29:59,500 --> 00:30:03,760
And then we've got Onan over there

614
00:30:03,860 --> 00:30:07,800
actually doing some of the braiding.

615
00:30:07,900 --> 00:30:10,000
And now Simon's doing some of the braiding too.

616
00:30:10,100 --> 00:30:12,720
Claudio and I are shaping strands.

617
00:30:16,720 --> 00:30:19,560
The word challah refers to the portion of a baker's bread

618
00:30:19,660 --> 00:30:21,420
that in ancient times was set aside

619
00:30:21,520 --> 00:30:23,320
and donated to the temple.

620
00:30:23,420 --> 00:30:25,060
That donation is symbolized today

621
00:30:25,061 --> 00:30:30,060
by a ritual in which the baker throws a small portion of the dough into the oven as a sacrifice.

622
00:30:37,060 --> 00:30:40,060
Along with the loaves of challah are the special orders.

623
00:30:40,061 --> 00:30:46,060
Simon is going to be making an eight-pound twist for us tonight, and he's using the egg dough.

624
00:30:46,061 --> 00:30:55,060
Some of the larger ceremonial events like a baby naming or an upshrin when a child turns three years old

625
00:30:55,061 --> 00:30:58,160
A boy has his first hair cut.

626
00:30:58,260 --> 00:31:01,660
Weddings, bar and bat mitzvahs.

627
00:31:01,760 --> 00:31:03,060
Ceremonial challahs will be used.

628
00:31:06,400 --> 00:31:08,100
Over the next eight hours, not only

629
00:31:08,200 --> 00:31:11,580
are 1,100 loaves of challah woven and baked,

630
00:31:11,680 --> 00:31:13,140
but all the rest of the bakery's goods

631
00:31:13,240 --> 00:31:14,180
are finished as well.

632
00:31:17,840 --> 00:31:23,280
And a tradition thousands of years old continues.

633
00:31:23,281 --> 00:31:25,080
Tradition is also at work in the making

634
00:31:25,180 --> 00:31:28,780
of this ancient form of bread, which is also the favorite bread

635
00:31:28,880 --> 00:31:30,180
of America's astronauts.

636
00:31:30,280 --> 00:31:31,480
9-year-old, discovery.

637
00:31:34,500 --> 00:31:38,120
2, 1, and liftoff of the space ship.

638
00:31:38,220 --> 00:31:40,640
If you were an astronaut, what kind of bread

639
00:31:40,740 --> 00:31:43,340
would you take on a space mission?

640
00:31:43,440 --> 00:31:45,740
When you consider that crumbs would be a terrible nuisance

641
00:31:45,840 --> 00:31:49,780
in zero gravity, it's easy to see why the flour tortilla

642
00:31:49,880 --> 00:31:53,280
is NASA's favorite space bread.

643
00:31:53,281 --> 00:31:56,380
La Reyna Tortilla Factory in East Los Angeles, California,

644
00:31:56,480 --> 00:32:00,380
turns out over a million flour tortillas every day.

645
00:32:00,480 --> 00:32:02,820
To feed this nearly non-stop production,

646
00:32:02,920 --> 00:32:06,820
bulk flour and vegetable oil are stored in outdoor silos

647
00:32:06,920 --> 00:32:10,040
and pumped into the mixing stations inside.

648
00:32:10,140 --> 00:32:13,340
La Reyna mixes the dough to their client specifications,

649
00:32:13,440 --> 00:32:17,140
adding baking soda or yeast, flavorings, and in some cases,

650
00:32:17,240 --> 00:32:21,040
enzymes to extend the shelf life from the normal five days

651
00:32:21,041 --> 00:32:25,380
to 45 days or more for overseas clients.

652
00:32:25,480 --> 00:32:27,820
Workers drop the mixed dough through holes in the second

653
00:32:27,920 --> 00:32:31,120
floor to one of the 15 baking lines.

654
00:32:31,220 --> 00:32:33,920
This particular line is a hand-stretch line.

655
00:32:34,020 --> 00:32:37,760
So the dough that you saw being made in the mixers

656
00:32:37,860 --> 00:32:41,420
comes down to a rounder divider, and then it's

657
00:32:41,520 --> 00:32:44,660
proofed or aerated in these trays.

658
00:32:44,760 --> 00:32:47,060
And after they go from the trays, they're

659
00:32:47,061 --> 00:32:51,700
They're defted with flour and put into kind of like a rolling

660
00:32:51,701 --> 00:32:53,800
pin kind of process.

661
00:32:53,801 --> 00:32:58,200
That goes through two kind of oval one way and oval the other

662
00:32:58,201 --> 00:32:59,200
way.

663
00:32:59,201 --> 00:33:03,000
So the ladies have to kind of make it round, obviously, and

664
00:33:03,001 --> 00:33:04,820
then put it through the system.

665
00:33:04,821 --> 00:33:09,120
It goes through the oven for about 30 seconds at 300 degrees

666
00:33:09,121 --> 00:33:13,460
or so, then goes through the cooling conveyors.

667
00:33:13,461 --> 00:33:16,160
Much of the machinery at La Reina was adapted from other

668
00:33:16,161 --> 00:33:20,740
tools like pizza rollers by company founder Mauro Robles.

669
00:33:20,840 --> 00:33:22,740
My father came to the United States

670
00:33:22,840 --> 00:33:25,240
as an immigrant from Mexico.

671
00:33:25,340 --> 00:33:28,680
He met my mom at a small tortilla factory where he

672
00:33:28,780 --> 00:33:34,640
worked, and that's how he got the idea of making tortillas.

673
00:33:34,740 --> 00:33:35,640
What do you think, Dad?

674
00:33:35,740 --> 00:33:36,740
How does that look to you?

675
00:33:36,840 --> 00:33:38,820
It's good because they have a way to...

676
00:33:38,920 --> 00:33:41,080
He went from corn tortillas at that factory

677
00:33:41,180 --> 00:33:44,020
to flour tortillas in his own business,

678
00:33:44,021 --> 00:33:47,760
And that's how Lorena started.

679
00:33:47,860 --> 00:33:50,660
The first tortillas were made of ground corn,

680
00:33:50,760 --> 00:33:54,500
a grain that was hybridized from wild grains around 3000 BC

681
00:33:54,600 --> 00:33:56,660
in Central America.

682
00:33:56,760 --> 00:33:59,540
Corn tortillas remain the bread of choice there.

683
00:33:59,640 --> 00:34:04,140
In Mexico, over 300 million are consumed every day.

684
00:34:04,240 --> 00:34:06,980
Today, Lorena makes equal numbers of flour and corn

685
00:34:07,080 --> 00:34:09,280
tortillas, but nearly all their corn tortillas

686
00:34:09,380 --> 00:34:11,780
are made into corn chips.

687
00:34:11,781 --> 00:34:14,520
They ship their wheat flour tortillas to all corners of the

688
00:34:14,620 --> 00:34:18,520
U.S., as well as to Europe, Japan, and China.

689
00:34:18,620 --> 00:34:21,560
While tortillas can be packed and shipped across the continent,

690
00:34:21,660 --> 00:34:24,400
other breads are made to be eaten the same day they are

691
00:34:24,500 --> 00:34:25,820
baked.

692
00:34:25,920 --> 00:34:29,860
At Masab, an Ethiopian restaurant in Los Angeles, California,

693
00:34:29,960 --> 00:34:33,440
customers are served one of the healthiest breads in the world,

694
00:34:33,540 --> 00:34:36,740
injera, made daily on the premises.

695
00:34:36,741 --> 00:34:42,640
This is one of the Ethiopian traditional restaurants.

696
00:34:42,740 --> 00:34:46,120
We serve all our gasses with injera, what we call it.

697
00:34:46,220 --> 00:34:48,460
Injera is made with teff.

698
00:34:48,560 --> 00:34:51,360
Teff is the world's smallest grain.

699
00:34:51,460 --> 00:34:54,500
In fact, its name means easy to lose.

700
00:34:54,600 --> 00:34:59,160
It takes 125 grains of teff to equal one grain of wheat.

701
00:34:59,260 --> 00:35:01,500
But these tiny grains, grown almost entirely

702
00:35:01,600 --> 00:35:04,660
in the Ethiopian highlands, are packed with vitamins

703
00:35:04,661 --> 00:35:08,900
with vitamins and minerals and are gluten-free.

704
00:35:08,901 --> 00:35:12,000
Once it's cooked and cooled, the spongy pancake-like bread

705
00:35:12,001 --> 00:35:13,060
has several uses.

706
00:35:17,760 --> 00:35:21,820
Once they prepare stews, and they prepare it

707
00:35:21,821 --> 00:35:25,140
and bring it in a traditional manner with big plates

708
00:35:25,141 --> 00:35:28,800
and everybody's around there, and take a piece of injera

709
00:35:28,801 --> 00:35:33,900
and scoop on injera so to eat the stews.

710
00:35:33,901 --> 00:35:35,700
So we don't use no use stencils.

711
00:35:35,701 --> 00:35:37,300
Everybody use hands.

712
00:35:37,301 --> 00:35:39,900
That's how we use it traditionally.

713
00:35:39,901 --> 00:35:43,540
One special Ethiopian ritual is called gershaw.

714
00:35:43,541 --> 00:35:46,340
It's considered a friendly gesture to feed a friend,

715
00:35:46,341 --> 00:35:47,740
depending on the size of the bite.

716
00:35:51,320 --> 00:35:55,920
While injera is part of the social fabric of Ethiopia,

717
00:35:55,921 --> 00:35:58,460
bagels have evolved from European roll

718
00:35:58,461 --> 00:36:00,100
to American phenomenon.

719
00:36:00,101 --> 00:36:01,400
I like it warm.

720
00:36:01,401 --> 00:36:02,420
Some people say hot.

721
00:36:02,421 --> 00:36:05,520
I don't think a hot bagel is good, but a warm bagel, you know,

722
00:36:05,560 --> 00:36:08,460
that's been baked and is warm, looks out of the crunch,

723
00:36:08,600 --> 00:36:09,300
that's what I like.

724
00:36:11,800 --> 00:36:13,960
Richard Friedman knows about bagels.

725
00:36:14,800 --> 00:36:18,060
He owns Brooklyn Bagel Bakery in downtown Los Angeles

726
00:36:18,061 --> 00:36:20,340
and has been around bagels all his life.

727
00:36:20,680 --> 00:36:25,240
My grandfather came to this country in the early 1900s as a baker.

728
00:36:25,740 --> 00:36:27,240
He started the Bagel Bakers Union.

729
00:36:28,640 --> 00:36:31,220
It was one of New York's most exclusive unions,

730
00:36:31,221 --> 00:36:34,020
Only sons of members could become apprentices,

731
00:36:34,120 --> 00:36:35,220
like Richard's father.

732
00:36:35,320 --> 00:36:37,020
They rolled out strips of dough,

733
00:36:37,120 --> 00:36:39,020
and then that dough was wound around your fingers

734
00:36:39,120 --> 00:36:40,420
and cleaved to the bench.

735
00:36:40,520 --> 00:36:42,960
But that ended in the 60s with a machine

736
00:36:43,060 --> 00:36:45,160
that could form some 400 bagels an hour.

737
00:36:45,260 --> 00:36:47,460
The front part of the machine is called a divider.

738
00:36:47,560 --> 00:36:50,080
What that does, it cuts off the desired piece.

739
00:36:50,180 --> 00:36:52,480
And then that piece comes down the belt

740
00:36:52,580 --> 00:36:56,020
and winds around the mandrel and actually forms a circle.

741
00:36:56,120 --> 00:36:58,720
And that's called the former.

742
00:36:58,820 --> 00:37:00,880
Bagels are made with the same weakening

743
00:37:00,881 --> 00:37:03,980
flour as most breads, so it's the next step that really makes

744
00:37:04,080 --> 00:37:05,520
a bagel a bagel.

745
00:37:05,620 --> 00:37:07,280
Bagels are cooked in boiling water,

746
00:37:07,380 --> 00:37:08,780
which makes them unique.

747
00:37:08,880 --> 00:37:12,220
It stops the yeast action and also puts a shine on the bagel.

748
00:37:12,320 --> 00:37:14,900
It's the only bread product probably that is cooked in

749
00:37:15,000 --> 00:37:17,860
boiling water before it's baked.

750
00:37:17,960 --> 00:37:20,760
Bagels are said to have been invented by an Austrian baker

751
00:37:20,860 --> 00:37:25,000
in 1683 who wanted to give a gift to the king of Poland.

752
00:37:25,100 --> 00:37:28,600
The king was a great horseman, so the baker made a roll in the

753
00:37:28,700 --> 00:37:30,880
form of a stirrup.

754
00:37:30,881 --> 00:37:34,280
Today, Brooklyn Bagels bakes over two dozen different kinds.

755
00:37:34,380 --> 00:37:35,780
The water bagel, the plain bagel,

756
00:37:35,880 --> 00:37:36,880
is still the most popular.

757
00:37:36,980 --> 00:37:39,380
And the second most popular would be onion.

758
00:37:39,480 --> 00:37:41,920
And probably after that, cinnamon raisin

759
00:37:42,020 --> 00:37:44,120
has become very popular.

760
00:37:44,220 --> 00:37:46,420
We do not make spinach and cheese.

761
00:37:46,520 --> 00:37:47,640
We draw the lines of work.

762
00:37:47,740 --> 00:37:51,040
But we're making a lot more than they made years ago.

763
00:37:51,140 --> 00:37:53,140
Yeah, if my father would see this today,

764
00:37:53,240 --> 00:37:55,940
he would probably say chocolate chip and strawberry.

765
00:37:56,040 --> 00:37:57,140
It wouldn't fly.

766
00:37:57,240 --> 00:38:00,040
Tradition may be out the window in bagel shops.

767
00:38:00,041 --> 00:38:05,580
But it's returning in the form of handmade artisan breads.

768
00:38:05,680 --> 00:38:07,780
And American bakers are in the vanguard.

769
00:38:13,280 --> 00:38:15,380
White bread may still be the biggest selling bread

770
00:38:15,480 --> 00:38:18,400
in America, but there is also a growing movement

771
00:38:18,500 --> 00:38:22,000
toward natural whole grains and handmade breads.

772
00:38:22,100 --> 00:38:24,340
It's called the artisan bread movement,

773
00:38:24,440 --> 00:38:26,840
and it began across the bay from San Francisco

774
00:38:26,940 --> 00:38:29,200
in Berkeley, California.

775
00:38:29,201 --> 00:38:31,240
One of the first to open an artisan bread shop

776
00:38:31,340 --> 00:38:33,800
was Steve Sullivan at Acme Bread Company.

777
00:38:33,900 --> 00:38:36,840
Bread in the 70s was increasingly made faster

778
00:38:36,940 --> 00:38:40,940
and faster and faster, with more and more leavening, more

779
00:38:41,040 --> 00:38:43,940
and more dough conditioners, less and less time for the dough

780
00:38:44,040 --> 00:38:48,260
to develop flavor, and consequently, texture.

781
00:38:48,360 --> 00:38:50,860
Sullivan's bakery emphasized handmade breads

782
00:38:50,960 --> 00:38:53,800
with natural ingredients and no preservatives.

783
00:38:53,900 --> 00:38:55,400
That meant that people had to come daily

784
00:38:55,500 --> 00:38:58,660
to get their fresh baked bread, and they did.

785
00:38:58,661 --> 00:39:00,700
The sales area in the bakery was so small

786
00:39:00,800 --> 00:39:02,900
that people had to line up outside.

787
00:39:03,000 --> 00:39:05,500
Sullivan had found the right recipe,

788
00:39:05,600 --> 00:39:08,400
and the movement spread, inspiring

789
00:39:08,500 --> 00:39:10,600
dozens of similar small bakeries,

790
00:39:10,700 --> 00:39:13,100
like artisan bakers of Sonoma, California,

791
00:39:13,200 --> 00:39:15,820
just an hour north of Berkeley.

792
00:39:15,920 --> 00:39:19,180
Here, owner Craig Ponsford works in his bakery laboratory

793
00:39:19,280 --> 00:39:23,160
to solve the mysteries of bread without chemical additives.

794
00:39:23,260 --> 00:39:25,860
I've been able to get up to 10-day shelf life

795
00:39:25,960 --> 00:39:28,660
on a loaf of bread, for example, with an almond.

796
00:39:28,661 --> 00:39:32,500
solution. The combination of these things will inhibit mold

797
00:39:32,600 --> 00:39:36,000
and bacteria and maintain softness and freshness and

798
00:39:36,100 --> 00:39:39,000
things like this. That's been real fun for me, kind of

799
00:39:39,100 --> 00:39:42,700
still trying to maintain my goal of my original bakery is to

800
00:39:42,800 --> 00:39:46,120
feed people the healthiest product I can.

801
00:39:46,220 --> 00:39:49,120
Ponsford's passion for handmade breads inspired him to

802
00:39:49,220 --> 00:39:53,220
enter an international bread making competition in 1996 as

803
00:39:53,320 --> 00:39:56,560
a specialist in baguettes. To the shock of the bread baking

804
00:39:56,561 --> 00:39:59,800
bread baking world, his baguettes won first prize,

805
00:39:59,900 --> 00:40:01,260
beating even the French.

806
00:40:01,360 --> 00:40:03,660
It was pretty exciting.

807
00:40:03,760 --> 00:40:06,800
That led him to become advisor to the US baking team.

808
00:40:06,900 --> 00:40:08,100
That smells fantastic.

809
00:40:08,200 --> 00:40:10,800
Competing at the Coupe du Monde de Boulangerie,

810
00:40:10,900 --> 00:40:14,620
the World Cup of Baking.

811
00:40:14,720 --> 00:40:16,820
It's held in Paris once every three years

812
00:40:16,920 --> 00:40:20,220
to decide which nation has the best bakers.

813
00:40:20,320 --> 00:40:23,120
12 countries compete for the prize.

814
00:40:23,220 --> 00:40:25,860
Each three-person team must bake specific types of breads

815
00:40:25,861 --> 00:40:29,560
and pastries, as well as create an artistic display made entirely

816
00:40:29,561 --> 00:40:31,960
out of bread dough.

817
00:40:31,961 --> 00:40:34,960
Competition is fierce, and the bleachers fill quickly

818
00:40:34,961 --> 00:40:37,500
with the passionate fans who come to cheer for their country's

819
00:40:37,501 --> 00:40:38,600
bakers.

820
00:40:38,601 --> 00:40:41,300
Over 80,000 spectators pass through this hall.

821
00:40:45,040 --> 00:40:48,040
Bakers are in constant motion for eight straight hours,

822
00:40:48,041 --> 00:40:51,680
as they rush to mix, knead, and bake the required 60 baguettes,

823
00:40:51,681 --> 00:40:55,620
country breads, original recipes, and assorted pastries.

824
00:40:55,621 --> 00:40:58,320
as well as the spectacular artistic displays that reflect

825
00:40:58,420 --> 00:40:59,820
the national pride of the bakers.

826
00:41:06,560 --> 00:41:08,560
The judges grade on the quality of the bake,

827
00:41:08,660 --> 00:41:10,900
the originality of the breads and pastries,

828
00:41:11,000 --> 00:41:14,140
and the artistry of the bread display.

829
00:41:14,240 --> 00:41:16,200
Sometimes there are real surprises,

830
00:41:16,300 --> 00:41:20,540
like in 2002, when the host French team burned the baguettes

831
00:41:20,640 --> 00:41:22,840
and the Japanese team took first place.

832
00:41:25,620 --> 00:41:32,620
The American team has performed remarkably well over the years,

833
00:41:32,621 --> 00:41:38,620
winning the silver in 2002 and the gold cup twice in 1998 and

834
00:41:38,621 --> 00:41:39,620
2005.

835
00:41:39,621 --> 00:41:43,620
Back in the States, the next generation of bakers is studying

836
00:41:43,621 --> 00:41:45,620
to be even better than their mentors.

837
00:41:45,621 --> 00:41:49,620
This is the San Francisco Baking Institute, the nation's preeminent

838
00:41:49,621 --> 00:41:52,620
school for all natural artists in baking.

839
00:41:52,621 --> 00:41:54,860
for all natural artisan baking.

840
00:41:54,960 --> 00:41:59,160
Founder Michel Souas saw a need for this in the early 1990s.

841
00:41:59,260 --> 00:42:01,420
The artisan movement started at the same time

842
00:42:01,520 --> 00:42:04,060
the good food came to the restaurant.

843
00:42:04,160 --> 00:42:07,960
So chefs start to request better bread for the table.

844
00:42:08,060 --> 00:42:10,380
And so it was a teamwork between the restaurant

845
00:42:10,480 --> 00:42:12,180
industry and the baking.

846
00:42:12,280 --> 00:42:15,080
And the consumer who consumed those bread in the restaurant

847
00:42:15,180 --> 00:42:18,320
started to be interesting to find those in the supermarket

848
00:42:18,420 --> 00:42:19,580
and local bakery.

849
00:42:19,581 --> 00:42:22,980
So it was a constant movement with the quality bread

850
00:42:23,080 --> 00:42:27,220
that didn't exist before.

851
00:42:27,320 --> 00:42:29,320
By experimenting with sourdough starters

852
00:42:29,420 --> 00:42:31,820
and natural ingredients, artisan bakers

853
00:42:31,920 --> 00:42:35,320
are discovering ways to make tastier, longer-lasting breads.

854
00:42:35,420 --> 00:42:37,940
These miches that they're baking now

855
00:42:38,040 --> 00:42:41,840
will be fresh for at least a week at room temperature.

856
00:42:41,940 --> 00:42:44,140
There are no preservatives in them.

857
00:42:44,240 --> 00:42:47,180
The sourdough acts as a natural mold inhibitor,

858
00:42:47,181 --> 00:42:50,280
and it also helps to retain moisture within the bread

859
00:42:50,380 --> 00:42:54,620
so that it does retain its freshness.

860
00:42:54,720 --> 00:42:56,820
Though Suas was trained in France,

861
00:42:56,920 --> 00:42:59,260
he believes American bakers will be in the vanguard

862
00:42:59,360 --> 00:43:00,920
for developments in bread baking.

863
00:43:01,020 --> 00:43:03,100
How's the crumb?

864
00:43:03,200 --> 00:43:04,000
It's good.

865
00:43:04,100 --> 00:43:05,100
Yeah.

866
00:43:05,200 --> 00:43:08,000
We have no tradition where we need to follow.

867
00:43:08,100 --> 00:43:10,240
Like if you are a French baker, you

868
00:43:10,340 --> 00:43:13,280
have to follow with a strict rule to make a baguette.

869
00:43:13,380 --> 00:43:16,000
Here, we have rule, we have basis,

870
00:43:16,001 --> 00:43:17,260
But we improvise.

871
00:43:17,360 --> 00:43:20,200
We improvise what to do with it to make the best flavor

872
00:43:20,300 --> 00:43:22,500
coming out.

873
00:43:22,600 --> 00:43:23,900
Some of the friends should come here

874
00:43:24,000 --> 00:43:27,040
to relearn how to make good bread.

875
00:43:27,140 --> 00:43:30,120
Today, locally baked natural artisan breads

876
00:43:30,220 --> 00:43:34,180
account for 11% of all bread sales in America.

877
00:43:34,280 --> 00:43:38,120
And that number is rising faster than instant yeast.

